      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Patients with severe persistent asthma comprise only 5-10 % of the total asthma population, but account for a large proportion of asthma morbidity and health care expenditures. Among patients with severe asthma, higher costs can be expected in association with not well or poorly controlled asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To summarize the evidence concerning the epidemiology, burden, and heterogeneity of severe asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A literature search was performed to identify citations using the terms \"severe asthma\" and \"epidemiology\", \"asthma control\", \"asthma\" and \"heterogeneity\"."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Successful management of patients with severe asthma continues to be a major unmet need. One of the barriers to successful management is the heterogeneity of asthma. Asthma is not one disease; it is a disorder that can be subdivided into a number of different phenotypes and endotypes. A revised paradigm for asthma management, that entails categorization of asthma patients via use of \"biomarkers\", and prescribing targeted therapy, will supplant what has been a \"one size fits all\" approach to asthma management."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The novel approach to asthma management, in which therapy will be more mechanism-specific based on phenotype/endotype, offers the potential for improved asthma care outcomes - particularly for patients with severe persistent asthma who are not well or poorly controlled."
        }
      ]},
